.SH DESCRIPTION
.B DVDISASTER
provides a margin of safety against data loss on optical media caused by
scratches or aging media. It creates error correction data which is used to
recover unreadable sectors if the disc becomes damaged at a later time.

.SH TYPICAL USAGE

.TP
\fBdvdisaster\fP \fB-d\fP \fI/dev/hdc\fP \fB-s\fP
Scans the medium in drive \fI/dev/hdc\fP for errors.
.TP
\fBdvdisaster\fP \fB-d\fP \fI/dev/hdc\fP \fB-e\fP \fIcorr.ecc\fP \fB-s\fP
Verifies the medium in drive \fI/dev/hdc\fP against the error correction file
\fIcorr.ecc\fP without creating an image: all sectors must be readable and
match their CRC sums from \fIcorr.ecc\fP. Omit \fB-e\fP for augmented media.
The exit status is 0 only if no defects were found.
.TP
\fBdvdisaster\fP \fB-d\fP \fI/dev/hdc\fP \fB-i\fP \fImedium.iso\fP \fB-r\fP
Reads an image from drive \fI/dev/hdc\fP into the file \fImedium.iso\fP.
.TP
\fBdvdisaster\fP \fB-d\fP \fI/dev/hdc\fP \fB-i\fP \fImedium.iso\fP \fB--read-raw\fP \fB-r\fP
Creates an image as described above. Each sector's integrity is verified
by using its EDC and L-EC raw data. Only possible for CD media; otherwise
the \fB--read-raw\fP option is silently ignored.
.TP
\fBdvdisaster\fP \fB-d\fP \fI/dev/hdc\fP \fB-i\fP \fImedium.iso\fP \fB--read-attempts\fP \fIn-m\fP \fB-r\fP
Creates an image as described above. Defective sectors are retried at least \fIn\fP
times and at most \fIm\fP times. Recovery of defective CD media may improve when
combined with \fB--read-raw\fP. 
.TP
\fBdvdisaster\fP \fB-d\fP \fI/dev/hdc\fP \fB-i\fP \fIaudio.bin\fP \fB-r\fP
Reads an audio CD into the file \fIaudio.bin\fP (2352 bytes per sector)
and writes the cue sheet \fIaudio.cue\fP; see \fB--cdda\fP.
.TP
\fBdvdisaster\fP \fB-d\fP \fI/dev/hdc\fP \fB-i\fP \fImedium.iso\fP \fB-e\fP \fIcorr.ecc\fP \fB-r\fP \fB--adaptive-read\fP
Uses the adaptive reading strategy to read an image from 
drive \fI/dev/hdc\fP into the file \fImedium.iso\fP.
Reading will stop when enough data has been
gathered to repair the image using the error correction file \fIcorr.ecc\fP.
.TP
\fBdvdisaster\fP \fB-i\fP \fImedium.iso\fP \fB-e\fP \fIcorr.ecc\fP \fB-c\fP
Creates an error correction file \fIcorr.ecc\fP for the image \fImedium.iso\fP.
.TP
\fBdvdisaster\fP \fB-i\fP \fImedium.iso\fP \fB-m\fIRS02\fP \fB-n\fP \fI350000\fP \fB-c\fP
Augments the image \fImedium.iso\fP with error correction information,
expanding the image to no more than \fI350000\fP sectors. If \fB-n\fP is omitted the image
will be expanded to the smallest possible medium size (CD, DVD, DVD9).
Note the missing blank between \fB-m\fP and \fIRS02\fP.
.TP
\fBdvdisaster\fP \fB-i\fP \fImedium.iso\fP \fB-e\fP \fIcorr.ecc\fP \fB-f\fP
Repairs the image file \fImedium.iso\fP using the error correction file \fIcorr.ecc\fP.
.TP
\fBdvdisaster\fP \fB-i\fP \fImedium.iso\fP \fB-e\fP \fIcorr.ecc\fP \fB-t\fP
Verifies the image \fImedium.iso\fP with information from
the error correction file \fIcorr.ecc\fP.
.TP
\fBNOTE:\fP
Omit the \fB-e corr.ecc\fP options when working with augmented images in the examples above.

.SH OPTIONS
Action selection (at least one action must be specified):
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-read
Read the medium image to hard disc. Use \-rn-m to read a certain sector range,
e.g. \-r100-200.
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-create
Create .ecc information for the medium image.
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-fix
Try to fix medium image using .ecc information.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-scan
Scan the medium for read errors. If error correction data is available
(an ecc file given with \-e, or an augmented medium), the sectors are also
compared against its CRC sums. No image is written.
The last line summarizes the result. The exit status is 0 if all sectors
were readable and matched their CRC sums, and non-zero otherwise.
Whether a defective medium can still be repaired is shown by \-t after
reading an image with \-r.
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-test, \-tq, \-\-test=q
Test integrity of the .iso and .ecc files. When the
"q" option is given, only information is output
which can be gathered without fully scanning the files.
If the image contains MD5 checksum tags as written by
.B xorriso \-md5 on
(superblock, directory tree and session tags), the full test also
checks the image sectors against them. This works without ecc data.
.TP
.B \-u, \-\-unlink
Delete .iso files (when other actions complete).
.PP

Drive and file specification:
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-device device
read from given device (default: /dev/cdrom).
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-prefix prefix
prefix of .iso/.ecc file (default: medium.* ).
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-image imagefile
name of image file (default: medium.iso).
For creating, verifying and repairing RS04 error correction files (\-o file),
a cue sheet (.cue) or a Dreamcast .gdi file may be given instead, e.g. a disc
dumped by redumper with one .bin file per track. Its files are used as one
image with 2352 byte sectors, in the order of the sheet; pregaps which are not
stored in the files are not part of the image. A repair writes back into the
\&.bin files. Only BINARY files with 2352 byte sectors (AUDIO, MODE1/2352,
MODE2/2352, CDI/2352) are supported. Only the size of the last file can be
corrected by a repair; a missing or shortened file in between can not be
recovered.
.TP
.B \-e, \-\-ecc eccfile
name of parity file (default: medium.ecc).
When reading, fixing or verifying, \fIeccfile\fP may also be a directory;
the .ecc file in it whose fingerprint matches the medium or image is used.
With RS04, \fB-e\fP may be given several times (or a directory may hold several
matching .ecc files): fix and verify then combine all RS04 sets of the image,
including those of an augmented image.
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-ecc-target file|image
Specifies whether RS03 and RS04 should create error correction files
or augmented images (default: image).
.TP

Tweaking options (see manual before using!):
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-assume method1,method2,...
Assumes that the image is augmented with one of the given methods.
This enables an exhaustive search for method signatures
and might be helpful for detecting error correction information on
damaged media. If the image does
not contain the specified error correction information, a significant
amount of CPU and I/O time may be wasted.
.RS
Possible values are RS02 and RS03.
.RE
.TP
.B \-j, \-\-jump n
jump n sectors forward after a read error (default: 16).
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-redundancy n[unit]
Error correction data redundancy. Allowed values depend
on the method:

.RS
RS01- and RS03-error correction files
.RS
\-n x\ \ creates error correction file with x roots.
.RE
.RS
\-n x% creates error correction file with x percent redundancy.
.RE
.RS
\-n xm creates error correction file of approx. x MiB size.
.RE
.RE

.RS
RS01 error correction flles additionally support:
.RS 
\-n normal - optimized codec for 14.3% redundancy/32 roots.
.RE
.RS
\-n high\ \ \ - optimized codec for 33.5% redundancy/64 roots.
.RE
.RE

.RS
RS02 images:
.RS
\-n CD\ \ \ \ augments image suitable for CD media.
.RE
.RS
\-n DVD\ \ \ augments image suitable for DVD media.
.RE
.RS
\-n DVD9\  augments image suitable for DVD9 media.
.RE
.RS
\-n BD\ \ \ \ augments image suitable for BD media.
.RE
.RS
\-n BD2\ \  augments image suitable for two layered BD media.
.RE
.RS
\-n BDXL3 augments image suitable for three layered BDXL media.
.RE
.RS
\-n BDXL4 augments image suitable for quadruple layered BDXL media.
.RE
.RS
\-n BDNODM\ \ \ \ augments image suitable for BD media burnt without defect management.
.RE
.RS
\-n BD2NODM\ \  augments image suitable for two layered BD media burnt without defect management.
.RE
.RS
\-n BDXL3NODM augments image suitable for three layered BDXL media burnt without defect management.
.RE
.RS
\-n BDXL4NODM augments image suitable for quadruple layered BDXL media burnt without defect management.
.RE
.RS
\-n x\ \ \ \ \ augments image using approx. x sectors in total.
.RE
.RS
\-n x%\ \ \ \ augments image with approx. x% redundancy. 
.RE
.RS
\-n xr\ \ \ \ augments image with x roots error correction data.
.RE
.RE

.RS
RS03 images:
.RS
Setting the redundancy is not possible due to constraints in the format.
The codec will automatically choose the size of the smallest fitting medium.
However, see --no-bdr-defect-management below.
.RE
.RE

.TP
.B \-\-no-bdr-defect-management
Enable bigger augmented images for BD-R (around +3%).
If you know you will use a BD-R medium and want to maximize the free space
for parity data, you can skip the defect management formatting before burning,
at the expense of potential coasters. Specify this option to tell dvdisaster
to produce bigger images that will only fit on BD-R media burnt without defect
management. Some burning softwares call it "formatting" the BD-R before burning.
Should the ecc header of such an augmented image be damaged, dvdisaster will
search for the error correction data using both BD-R layouts, so this option
does not need to be specified again for verifying or repairing the image.
.RE

.TP
.B \-m, \-\-method n
lists/selects error correction methods (default: RS01).
.RS
Possible values are RS01, RS02, RS03 and RS04.
.RE
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
print nothing except error messages and warnings, which go to stderr.
Useful together with \-\-json and the exit status (see EXIT STATUS).
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose n%
more diagnostic messages
.TP
.B \-x, \-\-threads n
Use n threads for encoding with the RS03 and RS04 methods. Use 2 or 4 threads for 2 or 4 core 
processors respectively.
On larger machines save one core for housekeeping; e.g. use 7 threads
on an eight core machine.
.TP
.B \-\-abort-file f
stop the current action (like the Stop button in the graphical user interface)
as soon as the file f exists. Useful for unattended runs.
.TP
.B \-\-adaptive-read
use optimized strategy for reading damaged media.
.TP
.B \-\-audio-offset n
RS04 only: read offset of a CD-DA rip relative to the rip the error correction file
was created from, in samples (4 bytes). By default the offset is detected
automatically when repairing; use this option if detection fails.
When reading an audio CD, the AccurateRip and CTDB checksums (see \-\-cdda) are
computed with this read offset correction; give the drive's AccurateRip read
offset for comparing them with the databases. The image itself is stored as read.
.TP
.B \-\-auto-suffix
automatically add .iso and .ecc file suffixes.
.TP
.B \-\-cache-size n
image cache size in MiB during \-c mode (default: 32MiB).
.TP
.B \-\-cdda
read a CD as a CD-DA image: every sector is stored with 2352 bytes
(audio samples, or the raw data sector including sync pattern, header and EDC/ECC)
into a .bin image, and a cue sheet with the same name and the suffix .cue is written.
This is done automatically for audio CDs; use \-\-cdda for mixed mode and CD Extra
discs to read their audio tracks as well. Audio sectors are read with C2 error pointers
if the drive supports them; a sector with C2 errors counts as unreadable (it is retried
according to \-\-read-attempts) and is stored as a dead sector marker (2048 bytes)
followed by 304 zero bytes. Drives without C2 support may interpolate damaged audio
sectors silently; dvdisaster prints a warning in that case. Adaptive reading is not
available for CD-DA images, and RS01, RS02 and RS03 error correction data is not used
while reading them. An RS04 error correction file for 2352 byte sectors given with \-e
is used to check the sectors against their CRC32, provided the rip it was made from
was read with the same read offset (otherwise the check is skipped).
Data sectors are checked against their EDC (Mode 1, Mode 2 Form 1, and Form 2 if
the sector carries one); a sector failing the check counts as unreadable like a sector
with C2 errors, so that RS04 can repair it.
CDs with Mode 2 Form 2 sectors (Video CD, Super Video CD, PlayStation, CD-i) are
always read as CD-DA images since these sectors do not fit into 2048 bytes. For Mode 2
(XA) discs, dvdisaster looks for them in 64 sectors spread over the data tracks; use
\-\-cdda if Form 2 sectors are rare on the disc.
After reading, the AccurateRip v1 and v2 checksums and the CRC32 of each audio track,
the AccurateRip disc id (which ends with the FreeDB disc id) and the CUETools database
(CTDB) TOC id and CRC32 are printed and written into the cue sheet as REM lines,
for comparing the rip with these databases using other tools; dvdisaster does not
look them up online. They match the AccurateRip database only if the drive's read offset
is given with \-\-audio-offset (the log and the cue sheet tell which offset was used).
CD-TEXT is written into the cue sheet (TITLE, PERFORMER, SONGWRITER, ...) if the drive
reports it; only CD-TEXT in ISO 8859-1 or ASCII is used, and packs with CRC errors
are skipped. See also \-\-test-and-copy.
.RS
Multi-session media (CD, DVD and BD) are always read as one image with the sectors
at their real positions. The sectors between the sessions are not read; they are
stored as zero-filled sectors and are not counted as read errors. Unreadable sectors
among the last two sectors of a session (the run-out of sessions written in TAO mode)
are reported separately.
Without \-\-cdda, audio tracks of a CD with data tracks are stored the same way.
Media with RS02 or RS03 error correction data can not be read in multi-session mode.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-catalog f
append the result of each scan (\-s) or read (\-r) to the catalog file f, so that the
state of a medium can be followed over the years. Each run adds one line with the
date, the medium fingerprint, volume label and size, the action and the numbers of
unreadable sectors and CRC errors, separated by tabs.
.TP
.B \-\-catalog-report f
print the history of each medium recorded in the catalog file f, and warn about media
whose number of damaged sectors is growing.
.TP
.B \-\-dao
assume DAO disc; do not trim image end.
.TP
.B \-\-defective-dump d
Specifies the sub directory for storing incomplete raw sectors.
.TP
.B \-\-drive-test
Prints a report about what the drive selected with \-d can do for dvdisaster:
vendor, model and firmware, the supported profiles and read features
(CD, DVD, BD, HD DVD, C2 error pointers, CD-TEXT, streaming),
the read error recovery mode page (whether the drive can deliver defective
sectors in raw mode), and a summary of the reading modes it is suitable for.
If a CD is inserted, user data, raw and CD-DA reading with and without
C2 error pointers are tried on the first data and audio sector.
Only read-only commands are sent; the drive settings are not changed.
The drive's read offset is not measured.
Please include this report when reporting drive related problems.
.TP
.B \-\-driver d (Linux only)
Selects between the sg (SG_IO) driver (default setting) and the
older cdrom (CDROM_SEND_PACKET) driver for accessing the optical drives.
Both drivers should work equally well; however the cdrom driver is known
to cause system failures on some ancient SCSI controllers. 
The older cdrom driver was the default up to and including dvdisaster 0.72.x;
if the now pre-selected sg driver changes something to the worse for you
please switch back to the older driver using \-\-driver=cdrom.
.TP
.B \-\-eject
eject medium after successful read.
.TP
.B \-\-encoding-algorithm [32bit|64bit|SSE2|AltiVec]
This option affects the speed of generating RS03 error correction data.
dvdisaster can either use a generic encoding algorithm using 32bit or 64bit 
wide operations running on the integer unit of the processor, or use
processor specific extensions.
.RS
Available extensions are SSE2 for x86 based processors and AltiVec
on PowerPC processors. These extensions encode with 128bit wide operations
and will usually provide the fastest encoding variant. The
SSE2/AltiVec algorithms will automatically be selected 
if the processor supports them and nothing else is specified by this option.
.RE
.RS
RS04 picks GFNI, AVX2, SSSE3 or NEON automatically; 32bit and 64bit select
its portable code and SSE2 its SSSE3 code. All variants create
identical error correction data.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-encoding-io-strategy [readwrite|mmap]
This option controls how dvdisaster performs its disk I/O while creating error
correction data with RS03. Try both options and see which performs best on your hardware
setting. 
.RS
The "readwrite" option activates dvdisaster's own I/O scheduler
which reads and writes image data using normal file I/O. The advantage of this
scheme is that dvdisaster knows exactly which data needs to be cached and preloaded;
the disadvantage is that all data needs to be copied between the kernel and
dvdisaster's own buffers. Usually, this I/O scheme works best on slow storage    
with high latency and seek times; e.g. on all storage involving spinning platters.
The "mmap" option uses the kernel's memory mapping scheme for direct access
to the image file. This has the advantage of minimal overhead, but may be adversely
affected by poor caching and preloading decisions made by the kernel (since the kernel does not
know what dvdisaster is going to do with the data). This scheme
performs well when encoding in a RAM-based file system (such as /dev/shm on Linux)
and on very fast media with low latency such as SSDs. 
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-fill-unreadable n
fill unreadable sectors with byte n. Useful for processing images which have been created by other tools. For example, ddrescue fills unreadable sectors with zeros; therefore \-\-fill-unreadable=0 should be used. Please note: Sparse files can not be processed with dvdisaster.
.TP
.B \-\-ignore-fatal-sense
continue reading after potentially fatal error condition.
.TP
.B \-\-ignore-iso-size
By default getting the image size from the ISO/UDF filesystem is preferred
over querying the drive as most drives report unreliable values.
.RS
However in some rare cases the image size recorded in the ISO/UDF 
filesystem is wrong. Some Linux live CDs may have this problem. 
If you read back the ISO image from such CDs and its md5sum does 
not match the advertised one, try re-reading the image with 
this option turned on.
.RE
.RS
Do not blindly turn this option on as it will most likely
create sub optimal or corrupted ISO images, especially if you 
plan to use the image for error correction data generation.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-internal-rereads n
internal read attempts for defective CD media sectors (default: \-1)
.RS
The drive firmware usually retries unreadable sectors a few times before
giving up and returning a read error. It is more efficient to set this to 0 or 1
and manage read attempts through the \-\-read-attempts parameter. Most drives ignore
this setting anyways. Use \-1 to leave the drive at its default setting.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-json file
writes a summary of the run as a JSON object to \fIfile\fP when dvdisaster exits;
use \- for stdout.
.RS
The report contains the program version, the actions, drive and medium
information, the image and ecc file, the sector counts of scanning/reading,
verifying and fixing, the error correction method and redundancy, md5 sums
where available, and the exit code with its meaning ("ok", "error",
"defects", "data_loss"). Keys which do not apply to a run are left out.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-mapfile f
write a GNU ddrescue compatible mapfile of the image to file f. Present sectors
are marked as finished (+), missing sectors (dead sector markers) as bad sectors (\-).
The mapfile can be viewed with ddrescueview or used to continue with ddrescue.
With -r or -s it is written after reading.
.TP
.B \-\-list-damaged-files
list the files of an ISO 9660 image (Joliet names are used if present) which contain
missing sectors, and how many missing sectors are outside of files.
Can be combined with reading (\-r) or used alone on an image file.
UDF-only images are not supported yet.
.TP
.B \-\-medium-info
Prints information about the currently inserted medium.
.TP
.B \-\-no-progress
Suppresses the progress percentage indicator.
.TP
.B \-\-old-ds-marker
Marks missing sectors in a manner which is compatible with
dvdisaster 0.70 or older.
.RS
The default marking method is recommended for dvdisaster 0.72 and later
versions. However images marked with the current method can not
be processed with older dvdisaster versions as missing sectors
would not be recognized in the image. 

Do not process the same image with different settings for this option.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-pause-after-errors n[:s]
pause reading for s seconds (default: 60) after every n read errors
so that drives which overheat on defective media can cool down.
.TP
.B \-\-prefetch-sectors n
number of sectors to preload during RS03 de-/encoding (default: 32)
.RS
Using a value of n uses approx. n MiB of RAM.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-raw-mode n
selects raw reading mode for CD media (default: 20)
.RS
The recommended mode is 20, which makes the drive apply its built-in error 
correction to the best possible extent before transferring a defective sector.
However some drives can only read defective sectors using mode 21, skipping
the last stage of the internal error correction and returning 
the uncorrected sector instead.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-read-attempts n-m
attempts n up to m reads of a defective sector.
.TP
.B \-\-read-medium n
read the whole medium up to n times.
.TP
.B \-\-read-raw
performs read in raw mode if possible.
.TP
.B \-\-regtest
modifies some outputs for better processing with the regression test scripts.
.TP
.B \-\-resource-file n
Specifies the path to the configuration file (default: $HOME/.dvdisaster)
.TP
.B \-\-rs04-memory n
RS04 only: memory budget in MiB for encoding and decoding (default: 4096).
It decides the codeword length of newly created error correction data:
longer codewords tolerate more scattered damage but need more memory or
more passes over the image. Any codeword length can be repaired with any budget,
a smaller budget only makes repairing slower.
.TP
.B \-\-sector-size n
RS04 only: sector size of the data when creating an error correction file:
2048 (default), 2324 (Mode 2 Form 2, e.g. VCD/SVCD video tracks) or 2352 (CD-DA audio
rips, see \-\-cdda).
.TP
.B \-\-speed-warning n
print warning if speed changes by more than n percent.
.TP
.B \-\-spinup-delay n
wait n seconds for drive to spin up.
.TP
.B \-\-time-limit n
stop the current action (like the Stop button in the graphical user interface)
after n seconds. What has been read so far is kept in the image.
.TP
.B \-\-test-and-copy
for audio CDs (see \-\-cdda): after reading, read the audio tracks once more and
compare the CRC32 of each track with the one of the image, like "test & copy" in
other CD rippers. A track with a different CRC32 was not read reliably, e.g. because the
drive interpolated damaged samples without reporting C2 errors; dvdisaster exits
with an error code in that case.
.TP
.B \-\-version
print version number and some configuration information.
.PP

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success; no defects were found (or none had to be repaired).
.TP
.B 1
Error; the action could not be carried out, e.g. because of invalid
options, missing or unusable files, drive or I/O errors.
This is also returned after printing the help text.
.TP
.B 2
Defects were found, but no data loss was detected: the medium or image
has unreadable sectors or checksum errors, or the error correction data
is damaged (\-s, \-r, \-t).
With \-f, all defective sectors have been repaired.
With \-t on RS02/RS03 data, a full recovery is possible.
With \-\-adaptive-read, reading stopped because the missing sectors can
be recovered with \-f.
.TP
.B 3
Data loss; the defects can not be fully repaired with the available
error correction data: \-f left unrepaired sectors, \-t predicts that
full recovery is not possible (or found no error correction data for a
defective image), or \-\-adaptive-read could not read enough data for a
full recovery.
.PP
\-s and \-r (without \-\-adaptive-read) and \-t with RS01 do not estimate
whether defects can be repaired and return 2 for any defects.
When several actions are combined (e.g. \-r \-f), 1 takes precedence,
otherwise the highest value is returned.

